  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Parliament Capacity Building</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This proposal seeks to strengthen the capacity of the Parliament in C&#xf4;te d&apos;Ivoire to effectively oversee public financial management and performance of government policies and programs.  C&#xf4;te d&apos;Ivoire is emerging from a long period of political unrests and armed conflicts which has resulted in a profound deterioration of its public institutions. The Ouagadougou Peace Accord signed in 2007 between the main belligerents has paved the way for the presidential and parliamentary elections which are planned for this year.  Already the reconciliation process has started to pay dividends with improved macroeconomic conditionscomma but there are significant institutionalcomma political economycomma and technical constraints which need to be tackled in order to sustain the economy growth and hence help break the spiral of conflict and fragility. In this contextcomma strengthening the Parliament is critical to ensure accountabilitycomma transparencycomma and participation in the management of public affairs and effective delivery of public services.  Indeedcomma parliamentary elections have not been held in Cote d&apos;Ivoire since 2000 and it is basically by decrees that legislative and public policy decisions are being rendered.  Parliament has not taken any active role in the budget formulation nor the control over the effective use of public funds. As expectedcomma this lack of executive accountability has led to corruption and limited development impact</narrative>
  </description>
